In article <arielCsuF7L.Jzp@netcom.com>,
Catherine Hampton <ariel@netcom.com> wrote:

>No, David, you don't get it.  We had been given permission to show the
>program to outsiders and talk about it with them. 

You, and some others, are interpreting the language of that note in a
tortured way to make your case. The intent was clear from the initial
sentence giving permission to talk about it _with other beta testers._

Showing it was a separate sentence. There was no permission to pan.
That is, you can "talk" but not "criticize". The distinction would
be patently clear to anyone who has served time in the military
as like a submissive automaton with no rights.

> I have in fact shown
>it to one friend and put off showing it to a couple of others til a more
>stable version came out.  One does not give permission to talk only if
>the comments are positive.

There was no permission to talk generally and publicly in so many words. I
think this to be an interpretation by those who wish to make Netcom wrong in
the matter. Netcom should have the opportunity to censor anyone who is
critical of them in their own forums. This is just good business sense.

>
>Second, her shell account was yanked.  That was out of line, as Bob
>stated, in any event.

This sentence to me suggests bad faith posting on your part, Catherine. Her
account wasn not "yanked." It was put on hold with a message to call Netcom,
a proper procedure. Within 10 minutes of her call, and she grovelled
sufficiently before the sysadmins and promised never to be
critical again (a sort of "virtual boot licking" if you wish), 
it was taken off hold.

You are entitled to your opinions on this, as I am to mine. But distorting
the facts poisons my campaign of trivializing free speech rights
and suggests a campaign against
Netcom for being tyrannical and oppressive. Unlike complaints 
against dorm food, such
material can cause commercial damage and when such distortion is deliberate,
is actionable at law. (Witness the recent massive anonymous mail smear campaign.
If Netcom finds out who is responsible, they should be drawn and quartered, and
any judge would agree).  I say that to illustrate the seriousness of careless
distortions in this discussion, and to suggest that Netcom would love to sue
anyone.

>
>You're continuing an argument the principals appear willing to drop.
>Why? 

Because there have been many distortions posted here which, even though the
principals have dropped, damage Netcom tremendously in my view (leaving
aside the question of whether they acted improperly, which of course
is totally irrelevant). Since bobr wants this
thing to go away, as a matter of good customer relations, he's not going to
call attention to the misstatements, distortions, and omissions of material
information on the part of Netcom.

> Your comments in this whole affair appear to be geared at blaming
>the victim, whatever the facts.

Give me a break. Her post made her the aggressor, not the victim. You seem
to be starting the reading of history where it suits you (with the hold on
her account) rather than where the incident began.

I've seen this "oh poor me, I'm the victim" before in those who won't take
responsibility for their own role in a matter, and their defenders. This
is like my "women who are raped deserve it" analogy I wrote about 
elsewhere.

As to why I'm interested in this, frankly, it's a matter of principle. It
seems to me it is Netcom which is being victimized by most of the posts
here, not her, and with all the attacks on Netcom, this group can use at
least one observer who sees the other side regardless of the truth of
the matter.

I have, however, said what needs to be said, so unless there are direct
responses to my posts which are so phrased as to require me to reply
(misrepresenting what I say, attacks on me for my views, etc.) I don't plan
to post anything further on this topic.

David


